

GU_4_Black_EtAl Transcription

Interview 1
Contributor: AB = Alexander Black
Fieldworker: AM = Angus Martin

[AM:] I’m going to ask you about these words now. You’ve mentioned one already. Maorach, that was just a general name for shellfish?
[AB:] That’s right.
[AM:] That was quite commonly used?
[AB:] Aye, it was that.
[AM:] That didn’t apply to any specific types of shellfish?
[AB:] No, no I think all shellfish was known as maorach.

[NOTE: maorach is a Scottish Gaelic term for shellfish. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] Now, [there are/?] two words I’m quite interested in, [skelf/?] and [skleef/?].
[AB:] Skelf?
[AM:] Are you familiar with both of these words?
[AB:] Well, a skelf. I’ve heard of a skelf. It’s only connected with wood as far as I know. A skelf is just a paring of the wood you see. Any sharp thing coming against the wood and you just take a skelf off it, you see.
[AM:] You haven’t heard of [skleef/?]?
[AB:] No.
[AM:] I understand that means a notch in a rock. [Skleef/?]. I think it possibly comes from the same Gaelic word as ’skelf’.
[AB:] …I’ve never heard it referred to as [skleef/?] at all.
[AM:] I heard it in Tarbert in Carradale, you know. I wasn’t sure what it was.
[AB:] They’re different you see. There’s more Gaelic up that ways.
[AM:] Now … there’s a word that I’ve heard is used on board the skiffs when they would be paring wood shavings to kindle a fire. There’s a name for these shavings.
[AB:] Aye, what’s this?
[AM:] Can you remember?
[AB:] Sgla. Aah, aye, it starts with an ’s’ too.
[AM:] [Slisheams/?]?
[AB:] [Sliseag/?], [sliseag/?], [sliseags/?].
[AM:] That’s right, [sliseags/?].
[AB:] That’s right, [sliseags/?].

[NOTE: sliseag is a Scottish Gaelic term which can mean a shaving of wood. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] Now … did you use the word [spleiter/?]? [Spleiter/?].
[AB:] Many a time.
[AM:] What was that?
[AB:] [XX] went through a tub of water and went [spleitering/?] up the way.
[Woman 1:] [XX] is called an awful [spleiter/?].

[NOTE: spliut is a Scottish Gaelic term which can mean splash/gush out. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] Now there was a word … that was, I think, connected with superstition to a certain extent. It was used about an unlucky person aboard a boat that would be called a certain name.
[AB:] Oh well now, I knew that. Oh dear goodness. Oh, I should have come here about 20 year ago. 
[AM:] Was it a bòcan?
[AB:] A bòcan – that’s correct. A bòcan. It was a word that was very commonly used in fishing.
[AM:] What type of people would it apply to?
[AB:] Just anyone who was unlucky. Or else maybe some fella coming off for the night you see and getting no herring and all the rest getting some. That’s the word you would say, the expression you would … you’re a bòcan. Don’t you come oot to [XX].

[NOTE: bòcan is a Scottish Gaelic term which can mean apparition, sprite, spectre or hobgoblin. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] If I could ask you about some words for fish. Now, there’s quite a range of words used for various stages of the saithe and its development. Can you remember some of these for the smaller stuff?
[AB:] Cuddies.
[AM:] Cuddies. And there’s one for the…
[AB:] The glaisean
[AM:] [Kleshin/?]. And now there’s a word that’s used. It’s more used in Tarbert. Piuchdaidh.

[NOTE: Piocach is a Scottish Gaelic term for the 4th year of a saithe. Source: Ellen Garvie’s Gaelic Names of Plants, Fungus & Animals. Glaisean is a Scottish Gaelic term which can mean a coal-fish in its 2nd or 3rd year. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AB:] Piuchdaidh
[AM:] Now, I thinks that’s …
[AB:] Piuchdaidh that’s just - you can say that piuchdaidh is a glaisean.
[AM:] Now was that used in Campbeltown or Dalintober?
[AB:] Och aye, aye aye. The glaisean ... the glaisean was supposed to be the proper name of it, you see.
[AM:] They are both Gaelic names.
[AB:] Are they? Oh well, I suppose any of them will do.
[AM:] The biggest stage was - can you remember that? The big …
[AB:] The stanelock?
[AM:] The stanelock, aye. Now the spotted dogfish had a name. The Gaelic name for that was used too.
[AB:] The spotted dogfish. Och.
[AM:] Was that the mùrlach?
[AB:] Aye, it’s the mùrlach but is that the spotted dogfish?
[AM:] Aye.
[AB:] Aye, the mùrlach.

[NOTE: mùrlach or murlaoch is a Scottish Gaelic term for the lesser spotted dogfish. Source: Faclair Dwelly and Ellen Garvie’s Gaelic Names of Plants, Fungi & Animals]

[AM:] It’s the one without the [XX].
[AB:] That’s right, aye. You could scrub your - scrub the floor with the [XX] back. It was as rough as a, as a, it was as rough on the back as a scrubbing brush.
[AM:] Would you skin it first or just let it dry out?
[AB:] No, no, just… What, do you mean for scrubbing?
[AM:] Aye
[AB:] Oh no, just catch the old dead fish.
[Woman 1:] But what would you dae for eating it?
[AB:] Eh? We weren’t going to eat it. It was just there, it wouldn’t have been et. [XX]
[AM:] Now there was a Gaelic word used there for the gurnard?
[AB:] The gurnard? No. I’m beat here.
[AM:] The oudan, an oudan 

[NOTE: cnòdan is a Scottish Gaelic term for the gurnard. Source: Ellen Garvie’s Gaelic Names of Plants, Fungi & Animals]

[AB:] That’s correct, that’s correct aye.
Woman 1: Aye, you know the name.
[AM:] Are you familiar with the word cleek?
[AB:] Aye.
[AM:] What senses of the word are you familiar with?
[AB:] A hook.
[AM:] Would a hook actually be called a cleek?
[AB:] Aye, well you see when we used to play the … play the cleek it was just a round ring, iron ring and the cleek, the cleek … it propelled the ring along the street as far as you’d like to go. 
[AM:] The actual instrument for moving the wheel to go round. Was there actually a hook on it?
[AB:] Aye, it is a hook. It’s a solid thing.
[AM:] It’s actually hooked on to the wheel?
[AB:] No, no. It was just, you birl your wheel first. It’s only just a light iron ring and when you just, when you get it going, just get your cleek in at the back of it.
[AM:] Aye, I’m too, I’m too young to know that.
[Woman 1:] I know about the cleek right enough. [XX]

[NOTE: cleek is a Scots term and one of its meanings is a hooked piece of iron used by children to guide their hoop. Source: Dictionary of the Scots Language. Clìc is a Scottish Gaelic term meaning an iron hook. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AB & Woman 1:] It used to be, it used to be an annual game. You’d have the cleek one time, one year, one part of the year and you’d have the jarries another part of the year. You know the jarries?
[AM:] Marbles.
[AB:] That’s right, that’s right.
[Woman 1:] You had all the old -fashioned names.
[AM:] Now just two or three words here. I’ll let you by.
[Woman 1:] I’ll surely manage to squeeze through if …
[AM:] Now I don’t know if this word has come in more into use recently or whether it was used a lot in your day. It was the word for the wee scallops - the queen scallops.
[AB:] The queen- queenies? 
[AM:] Aye.
[AB:] No, it never was used, would never have been used in my day. What was it they called them then?
[AM:] Creachans?
[AB:] Well, no. No, the creachans is … the creachans only came into my vocabulary about 10 years ago.

[NOTE: creachan is a Scottish Gaelic term for scallop. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] I suspected that. I think that’s more a Tarbert word or a … when they started fishing for them.
[AB:] That’s right, aye. I don’t remember what … what we used to call them. It used to be very thick up at Skippinish Bay there. Someone was getting the fill of the ring net of them. And maybe a fella would shout to you ’What did you get there?’ Instead of ... instead of calling them … what did you call them?
[AM:] Creagans.
[AB:] Creagans,  we’d tell them …
[AM:] It wisnae clams, no?
[AB:] No, no clams is a bigger shellfish. Strange the way it goes away out of your mind.
[AM:] Maybe it will come back to you, later maybe.
[AB:] Aye, afore you’re halfway out the door there it might, eh.
[AM:] Now there was a wee creature which resembled the porpoise. Is there a name in that?
[AB:] I know what you’re asking but I can’t remember. You just …
[AM:] Is it a pulag?
[AB:] Pollag! Pollag! The pollag!
[AM:] Now was that a porpoise?
[AB:] I think so, I think it was a younger porpoise. Of course they were always on the road. You see you get them especially around about the island there, [Devar?/] Island. And then you might not see one again till you’re on the Isle of Ross. They’re never in twos and threes as you see with the porpoises. There’s half a dozen at a time.

[NOTE: pulag is one of the Scottish Gaelic terms for a porpoise. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] Now, we were speaking about shellfish. There are some specific names for shellfish. Can you remember any of these … local names?
[AB:] Spute fish - is that …?
[AM:] I think that’s just spout fish. There are one or two here. The clappy dubh, are you familiar with that?
[AB:] Aye, that’s a bigger mussel, the cloopy dubh. You got them doon there about Achnan[XX]. Used to use them for bait.

[NOTE: clab-dubh is a Scottish Gaelic term for a mussel. The plural is claba-dubha. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AM:] Baiting lines?
[AB:] Aye.
[AM:] Would you gather them or dredge them up?
[AB:] Eh?
[AM:] Would you dredge them up or would you gather them by hand?
[AB:] No, you dredged them up. I mean you’re working out in maybe ten or a dozen fathom of water [before you could get them/?].
[AM:] Did you ever hear of [saul dubh/?]?
[AB:] [Saul dubh/?], aye.
[AM:] What was that?
[AB:] Well, that’s feeding for the fish.
[AM:] Did you have any idea what that meant? Did anybody ever … 
[AB:] [Saul dubh?/]?
[AM:] Aye … can you give an explanation of that?
[AB:] I think it was always [saul dubh/?] I heard them referred to. It’s big something isn’t it?
[AM:] Eh, well the …
[AB:] [Saul dubh/?]
[AM:] Well, dubh is black.
[AB:] Oh, black, aye.
[AM:] The Tarbert ones say it’s black eyes but the meaning you would expect would be black seed, like sìol, seed. I think it possibly comes from the same word. You’re familiar with sile too?

[NOTE 1: sùil in Scottish Gaelic can mean eye and it can also refer to a small cluster of herring in the water. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[NOTE 2: sìol in Scottish Gaelic can mean seed and it can also mean young of fish or sprat. (Sprats are small marine fish of the herring family.) Source: Colin Mark’s Gaelic-English Dictionary]

[AB:] Aye, aye.
[AM:] What … what did that eh..?
[AB:] It’s young herring.
[AM:] Young herring. A-ha. Was there any other fish that called by that name?
[AB:] That was called by that name? Not that I know of, no.
[AM:] Now, there’s two or three here. Now the winkie was called a winkie hare but was there any other name for it you heard of?
[AB:] Aye, when I went to the fishing at first.

[NOTE: winkie is a Scots term for a lighted buoy. Source: Dictionary of the Scots Language]

[Woman 1:] Do you take sugar?
[AM:] Eh, aye please.
[Woman 1:] How much? Two?
[AM:] Is it a wee cup?
[Woman 1:] Aye, they’re no very big.
[AM:] Eh, could I have one please.
[Woman 1:] One? Just one?
[AM:] One will be fine thanks.
[Woman 1:] Ah, right.
[AM:] We’re just about finished, hen. [XX]
[AB:] When I went fishing first it was the [learie/?].
[AM:] The [learie/?]? You didna hear of stuthaidh?
[AB:] Stuthaidh - aye.
[AM:] That wasn’t …
[AB:] No they didn’t call it that. That was put on to the lines. Aye.
[AM:] Did they use that in Campbeltown, stuthaidh. That word?
[AB:] Och aye, you’d hear an odd fella refer to it as stuthaidh. It wasn’t in general use you know.
[AM:] Was that just a buoy?
[AB:] It was a buoy with corks on it and maybe if you were on the lines, there were a flag on it, you see.
[AM:] That’s quite interesting.

[NOTE 1: upstüe is a Scots term (from Norwegian) which means buoy-rope/buoy at the end of a fishing line. Source: Dictionary of the Scots Language]

[NOTE 2: stuthaidh means refers to a large buoy usually made of the skin of a dog and used to mark the off end of a drift of nets or long fishing-line — W. Coast of Ross, Argyllsh. & Inverness-sh. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[AB:] Eh?
[AM:] That’s quite interesting. I wonder if these men that were using that word were Gaelic speakers? Or came off a Gaelic … 
[AB:] Och no, I don’t think so.
[AM:] Can you remember any of the men?
[AB:] They might eventually … no, no. What was that you said there?
[AM:] Eh, stuthaidh.
[AB:] Aye. No, I don’t think any of them’s Gaelic speakers.
