Interview 2 (Starts at 23.23) 
Contributor: Mrs Jess Campbell [JC]
Reporter: Angus Martin [AM]

[AM:] Now … you didna … cut peats at all? Was it coal?
[JC:] No, it was coal we burned really. We didn’t get … there weren’t many trees, you see. We never got … logs. Well, I’ll tell you what, they used to go over with the boat, over to Auchterferry and there was great wood came in there on the shore and they used to fill the boat with that and take it home and saw it up and we used that. And then there were whins at [Gruan na Creig/?] and in the [Garbost/?] and the [Barr/?] so sometimes they were as thick as that, the whins so they were beautiful. We used to burn them you see and then they were ready for … you burned them when they got withered and they were ready for to going in and you kindled your fire with that. My mother used to bake, with those whins you know - they made a lovely fire for baking.
[AM:] That would be the actual stems you would be using?
[JC:] Yes, that’s right. Oh, the jaggy bits were terrible. We left them.
[AM:] Would you just cut these with an axe or …
[JC:] Yes, they were very easy broken. Sometimes you broke them with your knees just or broke them with your hand. But of course if there were thicker ones you cut them with an axe. There was always plenty of instruments like that about the place.
[AM:] Would that be the … the job of the children?
[JC:] Pardon?
[AM:] Would it [have been/?] the children’s job?
[JC:] Yes, yes. We used to always go. And then the burning of the heather … the heather when it was burned - they called it in the Gaelic falaisg.
[AM:] Falaisg?
[JC:] And it was very thin of course, wee thin bits but oh it was lovely for kindling a fire. We never needed to be without it. There was a peat moss up at the end of the Craig. They called it peat moss but it was under water. But there must have been peat dug there once upon a time because it was quite peaty at the side. But I don’t mind of our folk ever cutting peat really.
[AM:] Did they ever …?

Interview 2 ends at 25.32

Interview 3 (Starts at 25.33)
Contributor: Hugh MacFarlane [HM]
Reporter: Angus Martin [AM]

[HM:] You know that gentleman I was on about.  Well he said that, you know [XX] down the road, down [XX] Street … there’s a place there. Well he said that was the Gaelic word for whelks. 
[Woman 1:] Oh, I see.
[HM:] [XX] That was what he said.
[Woman 1:] Well, might be right enough.
[HM:] And they used to go down and fill their boats down there and then that paid the rent for the year or what ever way they wanted to do these ... [XX] Well he said … that’s what he told me, that [peacach/?] was the Gaelic word for [whelks/?]. Whether that’s true, I don’t know.
[Woman 1:] Sgadan [naturally/?] of course that’s the Gaelic for herring. But what do they call the rest of the fish I don’t know. Mackerel and whitings and haddocks and …?
[AM:] Reannach, in Tarbert anyway, reannach, mackerel.
[Woman 1:] Mackerel.

[NOTE: reannach is one of the Scottish Gaelic terms used for mackerel. Source: Faclair Dwelly]

[HM:] Well, a gobag was a dogfish.
[Woman 1:] A dogfish, it was a dogfish right enough.

[NOTE: gobag is a Scottish Gaelic term for a lesser spotted dogfish. Source: Gaelic Names of Plants, Fungi & Animals]

[AM:] And a mùrlach too.
[NOTE: mùrlach is also a Scottish Gaelic term for a lesser spotted dogfish. Source: Ellen Garvie’s Gaelic Names of Plants, Fungi & Animals]
[HM:] And a [piuchdaidh/?], the [piuchdaidh/?] of course, the [piuchdaidh/?] was the, the saithe.
[Woman 1:] The saithe. Aye, that’s right.

[NOTE: piocach is a Scottish Gaelic term for a 4th year saithe. Source: Ellen Garvie’s Gaelic Names of Plants, Fungi & Animals]

[AM:] That seems to be the word they use it Tarbert. But doon this way, it’s more glaisean.
[HM:] Aye, glaisean aye.
[Woman 1:] The glaisean yes, that’s right, yes. We call the glaisean did we call …? No, we called it [stamelock/?] But was that the glaisean.
[AM:] I think that was the big ones
[HM:] That was the big ones, aye. The big ones.
[Woman 1:] They’re lovely, salted.
[AM:] These … these saithe you salted, would they ... would they be caught by hand lines? The saithe.
[HM:] No, no the rod.
[Woman 1:] The rods.
[HM:] We used the rods.
[AM:] Would that just be from the rocks?
[Woman 1:] You were out on the loch and you rowed all the time, and you drew it after you, you see, and then you caught them. And oh what a job it used to be taking them in. We used to … we weren’t good at taking them in, that’s us as young folk. We used to swing them round our heads, you know, and hitting somebody in the face.
[HM:] You had a board across the stern, you see, with a slot. You know, two boards nailed with a slot and the rod went in there, spread out. That was a [sgathach/?], wasn’t it.
[Woman 1:] That’s right, yes.
[HM:] That was a [sgathach/?] they called that boat and you didn’t get them just till the [dusk/?] came in and then you wouldn’t get one and then they would just come up when the [dusk/?] came in. You could follow them …
[Woman 1:] Yes, they just rowed very slowly through the [XX].
[HM:] [XX] and then take them in.
[Woman 1:] Yes.

