Interview 4 (Starts at 28.04)
Contributor: Caroline MacCallum? [CM]
Interviewer: Angus Mait[XX] [AM]

[AM:] You mentioned too that the fishermen used to go for coals.
[CM:] Yes [XX] - they went to Greenock for coal with a fishing [skift/?] and they took so many tonne - I don’t know how many tonne it would be. And then of course they couldn’t, of course they couldn’t get the fishing [skift/?] in near the shore because it ebbed up a good bit. You know, the ebb went out and … but when it was high water and that they took it in pretty near and then of course they just carried it in bags or in a barra, you know, into the place. Of course it was never put indoors - it was just left in a place. We called it the [cùlag?], I don’t know what that means really. It’s a Gaelic name but it was this place at the byre and you just emptied it in there. And the coal was beautiful … oh it was so cheap too and it was glossy, thon glossy coal.

[NOTE: skift is a variant form of skiff, a Scots term meaning a small type of fishing boat, like a yawl with oars and a lug-sail. Source: Dictionary of the Scots Language]
Break in tape at 29.05

Interview resumes at 29.13
[CM:] We had a well. The well was beautiful. It was as cold in summer as it was in the winter, with the spring just coming from the hill. And we cleaned that well - we had to clean it regular and we put those white chuckie stones, as they call it, in the bottom of the well and we would catch a trout in the burn and put it in to keep it clean. It would eat up all the weeds and things. That’s what we did.
[AM:] That trout would be there all year?
[CM:] It went round all the time, it lived there all the time. It was wonderful really. And then there was always a wee outlet for the water to go very slowly, you know, in case the water might get sort of stagnant. But it never did of course.
[AM:] Was that well actually … would it be … was it bricked?
[CM:] No, it was just … it just seemed to be stones right around it. [There were/?] some flagstones at the side, you bent down and then you carried … it was [stoups/?] you had. Did you hear of the stoups?
[AM:] Stoup? Stoup? [Oh aye/?]
[CM:] They’re in Auchindrain  - they’re in Auchindrain Museum I think our stoups, at [Silvercraig/?] I think Miss Campbell [Kilberry/?] came down so I think May gave her the stoups. It was a wooden … they made the stoups too of course, the men. It was wooden … it was just round … they were round and wooden. They were that size and then there was just a handle. The handle was fitted into each side. But my goodness when you went to carry you had to take two to balance yourself coming from the water. Oh, it was, it was really great. That was the stoups.

[NOTE: stope is a variant of stoup and is a Scots term referring to a wooden pail or bucket generally used for carrying water from a well. Source: Dictionary of the Scots Language]

Interview ends at 30.54

